course: dictionary
lesson: 7918
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. The word ray is a good example. Before you choose a meaning, read the words around it: they are its context, and they tell you which meaning fits. «A flask has two walls with almost no air between them, so heat can hardly cross, and shiny walls bounce heat rays back.» Here heat and bounce tell you a ray is a beam of heat.
explain: Now read «Picture a dentist's X-ray of a seven-year-old: the baby teeth sit in the mouth.» An X-ray is a special kind of ray that lets doctors and dentists see inside the body, such as bones and teeth. The words dentist and teeth are the clues.
explain: A ray can also be a sea fish, like a stingray. In maths, a ray is part of a line that starts at one point and goes on forever in one direction. Always ask: which meaning fits these clues? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: A ray can even be a feeling. «A ray of hope» is a small sign that things may get better. Words like this change their meaning when they are in a phrase, so look for words that go with ray, such as sunshine, hope, or X-ray.
fact: A ray is a thin line or beam of light or heat, such as the rays of the Sun.
fact: X-rays let doctors and dentists see inside the body, such as bones and teeth.
fact: A stingray is a flat sea fish with wide fins like wings and a long thin tail.
fact: In maths, a ray starts at one point and goes on forever in one direction.
fact: «A ray of hope» means a small sign that things may get better.
fact: «An X-ray doesn't hurt; you just keep still while the picture is taken.»
exercise: «A flask has two walls, and shiny walls bounce heat rays back.» What are the rays in this sentence? | Fish with wings | *Beams of heat that bounce off shiny surfaces | Small signs of hope | Beams of heat
exercise: What is a stingray? | A straight line that goes on forever | *A flat sea fish with wide fins like wings | A bright light from the Sun | A cheerful person
exercise: A friend says, «You're my ray of sunshine today.» What does she mean? | A flat sea fish | A line that goes on forever | *A cheerful person who brings joy
test: What does «a ray of hope» mean? | A flat sea fish with a long tail | A beam of light from the Sun | A part of a line that goes on forever | *A small sign that things may get better
test: In maths, what is a ray? | A fish with wide fins | A thin beam of light from the Sun | A type of X-ray machine | *Part of a line that starts at one point and goes on forever in one direction
exam: In «Big zoos have hospitals with X-ray machines, scanners, labs and operating rooms», what kind of ray does X-ray mean? | A line in maths | A sea fish | *A special ray that lets doctors see inside the body | A small sign of hope
